\title{The Tropical Limit of Attention Is Lossy but the Recovery Is Fast:\\
Maslov Gaps, R\'enyi Sandwiches and Key-Budget Bounds for Top-$k$ Attention}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{October 8, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We study the \emph{tropical limit} of softmax attention---the replacement of the softmax average by a hard argmax pointer---and its finite-budget relaxations, oracle top-$k$ attention. On a pretrained 24-layer, half-billion-parameter language model (Qwen2.5-0.5B), evaluated in full precision on held-out natural text at contexts $512$, $1024$ and $2048$, pure argmax attention retains only $0.364$, $0.289$ and $0.250$ of the reference behavior, getting worse with context; two keys recover $0.70$--$0.79$, four keys $0.88$--$0.91$, and eight keys $0.94$--$0.96$. The $0.98$-retention knees $\{16,32,24\}$ reproduce an independent earlier sweep exactly. A per-layer map of the \emph{Maslov gap} $g=\LSE(x)-\max_j x_j$ shows bulk medians of $0.17$--$1.86$ nats and isolates the last two layers as the only region far from tropical (medians up to $2.69$ nats, ninetieth percentile about $3.4$). A pre-registered prediction that the crystallization loss $\sum_i p_i(1-p_i)$ would be at most $1/4$ is refuted (layer means $0.34$--$0.97$).

We prove exact statements that organize these measurements: (i) the softmax weight of a key is $e^{-g_i}$, so the gap at the argmax is the min-entropy; (ii) a R\'enyi sandwich $1-e^{-g}\le\sum_i p_i(1-p_i)\le 1-e^{-2g}$, sharp on flat rows, so any row with gap above $1/3$ nat has crystallization above $1/4$; (iii) truncation lowers log-sum-exp by exactly $-\log$ of the kept mass; (iv) key-count floors $\tau e^{g}$ and $\tau^2/\sum_i p_i^2$, which calibrated on the measured maps force at least $13$ and at least $33$ keys; (v) a margin bound $(n-k)e^{-m}/k$ on top-$k$ loss; (vi) an argmax floor $1/(1+(n-1)e^{-m})$ that decreases in $n$ and vanishes; and (vii) a doubling inequality $R(2k)\le 2R(k)$ for sorted mass curves which the measured curve violates at every context, together with Weierstrass bounds for layer-compounded readouts.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

A decoder-only transformer, when producing token $n+1$, forms in every head of every layer a score row $x\in\mathbb{R}^n$ against the $n$ cached keys and outputs the softmax-weighted average of the cached values. The key--value cache grows linearly with context and is often the binding memory constraint on small accelerators. Compression schemes rest on the observation that attention rows are nearly sparse.

The most aggressive compression keeps one key per row, turning attention into a pointer. This is the \emph{tropical limit} of the softmax: the log-sum-exp
\[
\LSE(x)=\log\sum_{j=1}^n e^{x_j}
\]
satisfies $\max_j x_j\le \LSE(x)\le \max_j x_j+\log n$ and $\varepsilon\,\LSE(x/\varepsilon)\to\max_j x_j$ as $\varepsilon\downarrow 0$. This is Maslov's dequantization: the semiring $(\mathbb{R},\LSE,+)$ degenerates to the max-plus semiring $(\mathbb{R},\max,+)$, and the softmax distribution concentrates on the argmax.

Empirically, we push an oracle top-$k$ sweep down to $k=1$ on a pretrained model and measure, row by row, how far each layer is from the tropical limit. Theoretically, we prove exact inequalities converting those measurements into cache-budget statements, explaining a failed prediction, and showing that the measured quality curve is not a plain attention-mass curve.

\paragraph{Contributions.}
(1) A measured recovery curve from the tropical limit with exact cross-session replication of the knee chain $\{16,32,24\}$.
(2) A per-layer Maslov-gap map and crystallization map of a pretrained model, isolating layers 22--23 as the diffuse tail.
(3) Exact theorems: gap equals min-entropy; the R\'enyi crystallization sandwich; the log-domain truncation identity; gap and collision floors on key counts with calibrations; the margin truncation bound; monotonicity and vanishing of the argmax floor; the doubling inequality and its violation by the data; Weierstrass bounds for compounded readouts.

\section{Setting and definitions}

Throughout, $n\ge 1$ and $x\in\mathbb{R}^n$ is a row of attention scores. Logarithms are natural; gaps are in nats.

\begin{definition}[Softmax]
$p_i=e^{x_i}/\sum_{j=1}^n e^{x_j}$. The weights are positive, sum to $1$, and $x_j\le x_i$ implies $p_j\le p_i$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Maslov gap]
The Maslov gap of key $i$ is $g_i=\LSE(x)-x_i$. When $i$ is an argmax we write $g=g_i$, the gap of the row; $0\le g\le \log n$, with equality on the right exactly for a flat row.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Collision and crystallization]
$C(x)=\sum_i p_i^2$ is the collision probability ($=e^{-H_2}$, $H_2$ the R\'enyi entropy of order $2$). The crystallization loss is $K(x)=\sum_i p_i(1-p_i)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Kept mass]
For nonempty $S\subseteq\{1,\dots,n\}$, $M(S)=\sum_{j\in S}p_j$ and $\LSE_S(x)=\log\sum_{j\in S}e^{x_j}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Retention profile and knee]
For a profile $p_0,p_1,\ldots\ge 0$, let $R(k)=\sum_{i<k}p_i$. If $\tau$ is attained by some $k$, the knee $\kappa(\tau)$ is the least $k$ with $R(k)\ge\tau$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Argmax floor]
For $m\in\mathbb{R}$ and $n\ge1$, $F(m,n)=1/\bigl(1+(n-1)e^{-m}\bigr)$.
\end{definition}

In the experiments, \emph{retention} is a deterministic agreement score between the top-$k$ model and the full-attention reference on held-out text ($1$ means identical), and the experimental knee is the least $k$ with retention at least $0.98$. The profile definition above is the idealization in which retention equals kept mass; Section~\ref{sec:notmass} proves that the measured curve is not of that form.

\section{Experimental protocol and measurements}

\paragraph{Model and data.} Qwen2.5-0.5B (24 layers), natural text, contexts $n\in\{512,1024,2048\}$, measured on the held-out final $10\%$ of each sequence with data-free choice of $k$ and threshold; 32-bit floating point throughout.

\paragraph{Validation gate.} The custom forward pass was checked to reproduce the reference implementation exactly before any measurement.

\paragraph{Intervention.} In every layer and head, each row is replaced by oracle top-$k$ attention: keep the $k$ largest scores, renormalize, drop the rest. Random-$k$ and local-window controls from the preceding sweep were not rerun.

\paragraph{Predictions.} (P1) argmax attention is catastrophic and worsens with context; (P2) a few keys recover most of the quality; (P3) mean crystallization is at most $1/4$.

